// COMPILE PROBE. This file MUST FAIL to compile. That is the result.
//
// Claim: `as` is reserved and cannot be used as a variable name.
//
// This is compile-time behaviour, so no runtime assertion in the suite can
// reach it. The spec sheet carried it for months as an observation from a
// harness that is not in this tree, and it was withdrawn during the 2026-08-03
// audit for exactly that reason. A probe restores it as something a reader can
// check in five seconds rather than take on trust.
//
// Run:
//   byond-standalones\<build>\bin\dm.exe extras\compile-probes\reserved-as.dme
//
// Expected on 516.1666 and 516.1685:
//   reserved-as.dm:NN:error: missing left-hand argument to =
//   reserved-as.dmb - 1 error
//
// A build on which this compiles cleanly has un-reserved the word, which is a
// language change worth knowing about. Success is the finding here, not the
// failure: treat a clean compile as a result to investigate, not as the probe
// being broken.
//
// The control matters as much as the probe. If BOTH declarations below failed,
// this file would prove nothing about `as` specifically, only that the harness
// rejects something. `notas` is an ordinary name and must compile, so comment
// out the `as` line to see the file build cleanly.

/proc/control()
	var/list/notas = list()      // ordinary name, must compile
	return notas.len

/proc/probe()
	var/list/as = list()         // reserved word, must NOT compile
	return as.len
